{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.62,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["U11"],
      "pins": ["U11.15"],
      "nets": ["SDO1 (no-connect marker 'x')"],
      "observed": "On the 'NAVC Peripherals' sheet, the BMI088 (U11) accelerometer address pin SDO1 (pin 15) is terminated with a no-connect marker (x), while the gyroscope address pin SDO2 (pin 10) is correctly tied to +3.3V.",
      "expected": "The specification states the BMI088 must present I2C address 0x19 for the accelerometer (and 0x69 for the gyroscope). The accelerometer SDO pin (SDO1) has no internal pull-up, so to obtain 0x19 it must be tied to +3.3V, exactly as SDO2 is tied high to obtain the gyro address. Leaving SDO1 floating is inconsistent with the intended address and with the fact that the designer wired the other SDO pin.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "BMI088 U11: pin 15 'SDO1' terminated with a no-connect 'x'; pin 10 'SDO2' connected to +3.3V (NAVC Peripherals sheet)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Bosch BMI088, I2C addr 0x19 (acc) / 0x69 (gyr)"}
      ],
      "impact": "With SDO1 floating the accelerometer I2C address is undefined and, in practice, defaults low to 0x18. That collides with the MPRLS barometer (U18, address 0x18) on the same I2C1 bus, so the accelerometer (and likely the barometer) will fail to initialise / return corrupt data, breaking IMU state estimation.",
      "confidence": 0.62
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board matches the Hades specification except for the BMI088 accelerometer address pin SDO1 (U11.15), which is left unconnected although it must be tied to +3.3V to yield the specified 0x19 address; the floating pin risks an address collision with the 0x18 barometer on the same I2C bus."
}

